      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-save-connection-draft-1",
          "nodeId": "workspace-save-connection-draft-1",
          "portId": "root",
          "name": "Draft #1: Save selection as graph"
        },
        "title": "Draft #1: Save selection as graph",
        "purpose": "Review the transaction, recovery, and explicit Bridge authoring contract before runtime changes.",
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Draft #1: Save selection as graph\n\n## Purpose\n\nMake Save selection as graph a staged, recoverable operation. The current capture helper can build a parent graph, but saving should preserve the current workspace unless the user explicitly chooses to open the captured graph.\n\n## Product contract\n\nSave selection as graph saves a new parent graph from the selected projections and their internal workspace edges. It leaves the current workspace, selection, focus, camera, and dirty state intact after a successful save.\n\nOpening the captured graph is a separate action. If the product wants one command to do both operations, its label must say Save and open as graph. The save command must not silently replace the workspace.\n\n## Proposed transaction\n\n1. Snapshot the selected ready graph projections, their internal workspace edges, the current selection, focus, camera, and dirty state.\n2. Validate the selection before mutating the workspace. Report unsaved selected children and any other blocking condition with a concrete recovery path.\n3. Prepare the captured parent graph and its workspace-owned edges in memory. Keep child graph contents referenced by durable address; do not copy their contents into the parent.\n4. Open the destination save flow without replacing the current workspace.\n5. On cancel, leave the original workspace unchanged.\n6. On save failure, leave the original workspace active and retain the prepared capture as a recoverable draft or explicit retry state.\n7. On successful save, keep the original workspace active by default and report the saved graph address.\n8. If the user chooses Save and open as graph, focus the saved graph only after the save succeeds.\n\n## Bridge semantics for connection dragging\n\nAn export Bridge is the source graph's Port. It does not target another Port or graph.\n\nAn import Bridge is the target graph's Portal. The cross-graph Bridge relationship exists only when the export Bridge and its corresponding import Bridge are both present.\n\nDragging an export Bridge over a target graph is an authoring gesture. The gesture creates the import Bridge on the target graph, with the import carrying the reference back to the source export. The source export remains a target-free Port. The drag does not edit the export Bridge into a directed connection.\n\nThe browser proof must show the source export, the newly created target import, the reciprocal relationship, and the behavior when the target is invalid or read-only.\n\n## Edge ownership\n\nWorkspace-created cross-graph connections remain workspace-owned in the captured parent graph. Legacy child edgeReferences remain readable during migration, but the save contract must say which representation is authoritative for new workspace edges.\n\n## Dirty graph behavior\n\nThe browser proof must cover both selected and unselected dirty child graphs. The operation must never discard dirty work silently. The user-facing outcome should identify the graph that blocks capture and the action that resolves it.\n\n## Acceptance evidence\n\n- Success creates the expected parent graph and preserves internal selected edges.\n- A successful Save selection as graph leaves the current workspace, selection, focus, camera, and dirty state unchanged.\n- Cancel leaves the current workspace unchanged.\n- Save failure leaves the original workspace active and exposes retry or discard explicitly.\n- Save and open as graph focuses the captured graph only after successful persistence.\n- Dragging an export Bridge creates the corresponding import Bridge on the target graph.\n- The export Bridge remains a target-free source Port, and the import Bridge carries the target-side relationship.\n- Invalid and read-only targets leave the source export unchanged and explain the failure.\n- Reload preserves the saved workspace-owned edges and Bridge relationship.\n- The smoke records the exact destination and source graph addresses.\n\n## Open decision\n\nShould a failed save keep a staged capture shell available through an explicit retry action, or should it discard the staged capture and return only to the original workspace?"
        },
        "renderShape": {
          "kind": "markdown"
        },
        "visibilityRole": "browser"
      }
    }
